---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PeriodicTable from '../components/PeriodicTable.astro';
import { getEntries } from '../lib/catalog.mjs';

const total = getEntries().length;
---
<Base
  title="The Catalog of Complexity"
  description="A reference catalog of canonical complex systems — stars, storms, markets, languages — measured on one fixed panel of thirty attributes, with the evidence for every value in view."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1 class="page-title">The Catalog of Complexity</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      A reference catalog of canonical complex systems — stars, storms, markets, languages —
      measured on one fixed panel of thirty attributes, with the evidence for every value in view.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block prose">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Complexity science has spent fifty years describing what stars, cities, earthquakes and
      economies have in common. The catalog puts the canonical systems side by side on a single
      instrument: every class entry carries the same 30-column panel — quantitative values
      harvested from the literature, and 0–4 rubric scores made commensurable by worked anchor
      examples. Entries are classes, not instances: "tropical cyclones," with Hurricane Katrina
      as a designated evidence-carrying exemplar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="periodic-table" class="block">
    <h2>The systems, arranged by kind and by kinship</h2>
    <PeriodicTable />
    <p class="tiny" style="color: var(--ink-faint); margin-top: 18px;">
      {total} verified systems. Full listing at <a href="/catalog/">the catalog index</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block prose">
    <h2>Why you can trust it</h2>
    <p>
      Every value carries an evidence status — measured, derived, disputed, or honestly unknown;
      "unknown" is always legal, guessing never is. Every citation is annotated with what was
      actually read. Power-law claims meet Clauset–Shalizi–Newman-grade statistics or are
      labeled untested. Every entry passes four adversarial verification agents — citation,
      plausibility, cross-entry consistency, coverage — before promotion, and the whole panel
      has been stress-tested by blind re-scoring and robustness studies. Read more on the
      <a href="/methods/">methods</a> page, and see the claims we deliberately avoid on the
      <a href="/myths/">myths</a> page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>The views</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <a class="card" href="#periodic-table">
        <h3>Table</h3>
        <p class="card-summary">The systems arranged by category, on this page.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/catalog/">
        <h3>Catalog index</h3>
        <p class="card-summary">Every verified entry as a card, grouped by category.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/compare/">
        <h3>Compare</h3>
        <p class="card-summary">Overlay 2–4 systems on a radar chart across the 15 rubric scores.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/map/">
        <h3>Map</h3>
        <p class="card-summary">All systems embedded in metric space via PCA over the full panel.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
